icon":235},"Grind Jobs","\u002Fplatform\u002Freference\u002Fgrind-jobs","04.platform\u002F12.reference\u002F02.grind-jobs","i-lucide-workflow",{"title":237,"path":238,"stem":239,"icon":174},"Project Publishing Design","\u002Fplatform\u002Freference\u002Fproject-publishing-design","04.platform\u002F12.reference\u002F03.project-publishing-design",{"title":241,"path":242,"stem":243,"icon":33},"Sign-In Design","\u002Fplatform\u002Freference\u002Fsign-in-design","04.platform\u002F12.reference\u002F04.sign-in-design",{"title":245,"path":246,"stem":247,"icon":248},"Development Server","\u002Fplatform\u002Fdevelopment-server","04.platform\u002F13.development-server","i-lucide-server-cog",{"title":250,"path":251,"stem":252,"icon":253},"Supply Chain Security","\u002Fplatform\u002Fsupply-chain-security","04.platform\u002F14.supply-chain-security","i-lucide-package-check",{"title":255,"path":256,"stem":257,"icon":258},"Enterprise License","\u002Fplatform\u002Fenterprise-license","04.platform\u002F15.enterprise-license","i-lucide-badge-check",{"id":260,"title":213,"body":261,"description":877,"extension":878,"links":879,"meta":880,"navigation":881,"path":214,"seo":882,"stem":215,"__hash__":883},"docs\u002F04.platform\u002F10.system-migrations.md",{"type":262,"value":263,"toc":864},"minimark",[264,273,278,281,293,297,305,309,312,328,333,336,389,403,508,511,526,532,536,551,561,565,579,609,625,633,653,671,718,732,743,777,795,825,841,845,860],[265,266,267,268,272],"p",{},"The Kinotic platform uses the same migration SQL system as application developers to manage its own internal Elasticsearch indices. This page covers considerations specific to platform development — for general migration syntax, see ",[269,270,271],"a",{"href":114},"App Migrations",".",[274,275,277],"h2",{"id":276},"what-are-system-migrations","What Are System Migrations?",[265,279,280],{},"System migrations define and evolve the indices that the platform itself relies on — things like migration history tracking, system configuration, and other internal data stores. They run during platform startup before any application migrations and are scoped to the system project rather than a per-application project.",[265,282,283,284,288,289,292],{},"System migrations live in ",[285,286,287],"code",{},"kinotic-migration\u002Fsrc\u002Fmain\u002Fresources\u002Fmigrations\u002F"," and follow the same ",[285,290,291],{},"V\u003CN>__\u003Cdescription>.sql"," naming convention.",[274,294,296],{"id":295},"using-composite-types-in-system-indices","Using Composite Types in System Indices",[265,298,299,300,304],{},"OBJECT, NESTED, and UNION columns work the same way in system migrations as in application migrations. Refer to the ",[269,301,303],{"href":302},"\u002Fapps\u002Fpersistence\u002Fmigrations#composite-column-types","Composite Column Types"," section of the app documentation for syntax and examples.",[274,306,308],{"id":307},"deserializing-union-types","Deserializing Union Types",[265,310,311],{},"Elasticsearch stores UNION fields as a flat merged object — it has no knowledge of which variant a document contains. When reading documents back into Java, the platform needs to route deserialization to the correct subclass.",[265,313,314,315,319,320,323,324,327],{},"The standard approach is Jackson's polymorphic type handling, driven by a ",[316,317,318],"strong",{},"discriminator field"," stored in the document. This is the same ",[285,321,322],{},"kind"," (or similar) ",[285,325,326],{},"KEYWORD"," field you include in each union variant in the migration SQL.",[329,330,332],"h3",{"id":331},"example","Example",[265,334,335],{},"Given this migration:",[337,338,343],"pre",{"className":339,"code":340,"language":341,"meta":342,"style":342},"language-sql shiki shiki-themes material-theme-lighter material-theme material-theme-palenight","CREATE TABLE assets (\n    id KEYWORD,\n    item UNION (\n        Book  (kind KEYWORD, title TEXT, isbn KEYWORD),\n        Video (kind KEYWORD, title TEXT, duration INTEGER)\n    )\n);\n","sql","",[285,344,345,353,359,365,371,377,383],{"__ignoreMap":342},[346,347,350],"span",{"class":348,"line":349},"line",1,[346,351,352],{},"CREATE TABLE assets (\n",[346,354,356],{"class":348,"line":355},2,[346,357,358],{},"    id KEYWORD,\n",[346,360,362],{"class":348,"line":361},3,[346,363,364],{},"    item UNION (\n",[346,366,368],{"class":348,"line":367},4,[346,369,370],{},"        Book  (kind KEYWORD, title TEXT, isbn KEYWORD),\n",[346,372,374],{"class":348,"line":373},5,[346,375,376],{},"        Video (kind KEYWORD, title TEXT, duration INTEGER)\n",[346,378,380],{"class":348,"line":379},6,[346,381,382],{},"    )\n",[346,384,386],{"class":348,"line":385},7,[346,387,388],{},");\n",[265,390,391,392,395,396,399,400,402],{},"The corresponding Java model uses ",[285,393,394],{},"@JsonTypeInfo"," and ",[285,397,398],{},"@JsonSubTypes"," to map the ",[285,401,322],{}," field value to the correct subclass:",[337,404,408],{"className":405,"code":406,"language":407,"meta":342,"style":342},"language-java shiki shiki-themes material-theme-lighter material-theme material-theme-palenight","@JsonTypeInfo(use = JsonTypeInfo.Id.NAME, property = \"kind\")\n@JsonSubTypes({\n    @JsonSubTypes.Type(value = Book.class,  name = \"Book\"),\n    @JsonSubTypes.Type(value = Video.class, name = \"Video\")\n})\npublic abstract class Item {}\n\npublic class Book extends Item {\n    public String kind;\n    public String title;\n    public String isbn;\n}\n\npublic class Video extends Item {\n    public String kind;\n    public String title;\n    public Integer duration;\n}\n","java",[285,409,410,415,420,425,430,435,440,446,452,458,464,470,476,481,487,492,497,503],{"__ignoreMap":342},[346,411,412],{"class":348,"line":349},[346,413,414],{},"@JsonTypeInfo(use = JsonTypeInfo.Id.NAME, property = \"kind\")\n",[346,416,417],{"class":348,"line":355},[346,418,419],{},"@JsonSubTypes({\n",[346,421,422],{"class":348,"line":361},[346,423,424],{},"    @JsonSubTypes.Type(value = Book.class,  name = \"Book\"),\n",[346,426,427],{"class":348,"line":367},[346,428,429],{},"    @JsonSubTypes.Type(value = Video.class, name = \"Video\")\n",[346,431,432],{"class":348,"line":373},[346,433,434],{},"})\n",[346,436,437],{"class":348,"line":379},[346,438,439],{},"public abstract class Item {}\n",[346,441,442],{"class":348,"line":385},[346,443,445],{"emptyLinePlaceholder":444},true,"\n",[346,447,449],{"class":348,"line":448},8,[346,450,451],{},"public class Book extends Item {\n",[346,453,455],{"class":348,"line":454},9,[346,456,457],{},"    public String kind;\n",[346,459,461],{"class":348,"line":460},10,[346,462,463],{},"    public String title;\n",[346,465,467],{"class":348,"line":466},11,[346,468,469],{},"    public String isbn;\n",[346,471,473],{"class":348,"line":472},12,[346,474,475],{},"}\n",[346,477,479],{"class":348,"line":478},13,[346,480,445],{"emptyLinePlaceholder":444},[346,482,484],{"class":348,"line":483},14,[346,485,486],{},"public class Video extends Item {\n",[346,488,490],{"class":348,"line":489},15,[346,491,457],{},[346,493,495],{"class":348,"line":494},16,[346,496,463],{},[346,498,500],{"class":348,"line":499},17,[346,501,502],{},"    public Integer duration;\n",[346,504,506],{"class":348,"line":505},18,[346,507,475],{},[265,509,510],{},"When inserting documents, always write the discriminator value:",[337,512,514],{"className":339,"code":513,"language":341,"meta":342,"style":342},"INSERT INTO assets (id, item)\n    VALUES ('a1', { kind: 'Book', title: 'Clean Code', isbn: '978-0132350884' }) WITH REFRESH;\n",[285,515,516,521],{"__ignoreMap":342},[346,517,518],{"class":348,"line":349},[346,519,520],{},"INSERT INTO assets (id, item)\n",[346,522,523],{"class":348,"line":355},[346,524,525],{},"    VALUES ('a1', { kind: 'Book', title: 'Clean Code', isbn: '978-0132350884' }) WITH REFRESH;\n",[265,527,528,529,531],{},"If the ",[285,530,322],{}," field is absent from a stored document, Jackson will fail to deserialize it. Ensure every INSERT that targets a UNION field includes the discriminator.",[329,533,535],{"id":534},"conflict-validation","Conflict validation",[265,537,538,539,542,543,546,547,550],{},"If two variants declare the same field name with different scalar types — e.g. ",[285,540,541],{},"title TEXT"," in one variant and ",[285,544,545],{},"title KEYWORD"," in another — the migration executor will throw an ",[285,548,549],{},"IllegalArgumentException"," at mapping time. Fields shared across variants must have identical types.",[265,552,553,556,557,560],{},[316,554,555],{},"Known limitation:"," Conflict detection is shallow for composite types. If two variants share a field name with the same composite type (OBJECT, NESTED, or UNION) but different sub-fields, no exception is thrown — only the first variant's sub-field definitions are used and the second's are silently dropped. Until named type support is added to the migration DSL, avoid sharing composite-typed fields across variants. Scalar shared fields (such as a ",[285,558,559],{},"kind KEYWORD"," discriminator) are always safe.",[274,562,564],{"id":563},"data-streams","Data Streams",[265,566,567,568,571,572,578],{},"System data that is append-only and time-series — audit trails, events, telemetry — should be backed by an Elasticsearch ",[316,569,570],{},"data stream"," rather than a regular index, so old data ages out and backing indices roll over automatically. Create one from a migration with ",[269,573,575],{"href":574},"\u002Fapps\u002Freference\u002Fmigration-sql-grammar#create-data-stream",[285,576,577],{},"CREATE DATA STREAM",":",[337,580,582],{"className":339,"code":581,"language":341,"meta":342,"style":342},"CREATE DATA STREAM system_audit (\n    actor  KEYWORD,\n    action KEYWORD,\n    detail TEXT\n) WITH (DATA_RETENTION = '90d') ;\n",[285,583,584,589,594,599,604],{"__ignoreMap":342},[346,585,586],{"class":348,"line":349},[346,587,588],{},"CREATE DATA STREAM system_audit (\n",[346,590,591],{"class":348,"line":355},[346,592,593],{},"    actor  KEYWORD,\n",[346,595,596],{"class":348,"line":361},[346,597,598],{},"    action KEYWORD,\n",[346,600,601],{"class":348,"line":367},[346,602,603],{},"    detail TEXT\n",[346,605,606],{"class":348,"line":373},[346,607,608],{},") WITH (DATA_RETENTION = '90d') ;\n",[265,610,611,614,615,621,622,624],{},[285,612,613],{},"DATA_RETENTION"," is the native ",[269,616,620],{"href":617,"rel":618},"https:\u002F\u002Fwww.elastic.co\u002Fdocs\u002Fmanage-data\u002Flifecycle\u002Fdata-stream",[619],"nofollow","data stream lifecycle"," — Elasticsearch deletes data past the period (measured per backing index from rollover, not per document). See the ",[269,623,162],{"href":574}," for the full statement and the exact retention semantics.",[329,626,628,629,632],{"id":627},"every-document-needs-a-timestamp-and-java-needs-ceremony-for-it","Every document needs a ",[285,630,631],{},"@timestamp"," — and Java needs ceremony for it",[265,634,635,636,639,640,642,643,646,647,649,650,652],{},"Elasticsearch ",[316,637,638],{},"requires"," every document written to a data stream to carry a ",[285,641,631],{}," date field, and rejects any that don't with ",[285,644,645],{},"data stream timestamp field [@timestamp] is missing",". The migration adds ",[285,648,631],{}," to the mapping automatically — you neither declare it as a column nor can you, since ",[285,651,631],{}," is not a legal column identifier.",[265,654,655,656,659,660,662,663,666,667,670],{},"The catch is on the ",[316,657,658],{},"write"," side: ",[285,661,631],{}," is also not a legal Java field name, so a system DTO cannot carry it directly. Bridge it with Jackson's ",[285,664,665],{},"@JsonProperty"," — the ",[285,668,669],{},"ElasticsearchAsyncClient"," serializes through a Jackson mapper, so the annotation is honored on both write and read:",[337,672,674],{"className":405,"code":673,"language":407,"meta":342,"style":342},"public class AuditEvent {\n    private String  id;\n\n    @JsonProperty(\"@timestamp\")      \u002F\u002F Java field `timestamp` ⇄ Elasticsearch field `@timestamp`\n    private Instant timestamp;\n\n    private String  actor;\n    private String  action;\n}\n",[285,675,676,681,686,690,695,700,704,709,714],{"__ignoreMap":342},[346,677,678],{"class":348,"line":349},[346,679,680],{},"public class AuditEvent {\n",[346,682,683],{"class":348,"line":355},[346,684,685],{},"    private String  id;\n",[346,687,688],{"class":348,"line":361},[346,689,445],{"emptyLinePlaceholder":444},[346,691,692],{"class":348,"line":367},[346,693,694],{},"    @JsonProperty(\"@timestamp\")      \u002F\u002F Java field `timestamp` ⇄ Elasticsearch field `@timestamp`\n",[346,696,697],{"class":348,"line":373},[346,698,699],{},"    private Instant timestamp;\n",[346,701,702],{"class":348,"line":379},[346,703,445],{"emptyLinePlaceholder":444},[346,705,706],{"class":348,"line":385},[346,707,708],{},"    private String  actor;\n",[346,710,711],{"class":348,"line":448},[346,712,713],{},"    private String  action;\n",[346,715,716],{"class":348,"line":454},[346,717,475],{},[265,719,720,721,724,725,727,728,731],{},"This ",[316,722,723],{},"renames"," the field — the stored document has only ",[285,726,631],{},", so any query or sort must use that literal name (",[285,729,730],{},"timestamp"," does not exist in the index). For most internal stream DTOs this is the simplest correct choice.",[265,733,734,735,739,740,742],{},"If you instead want a friendly, separately-queryable field name ",[736,737,738],"em",{},"alongside"," ",[285,741,631],{},", keep your own field and add a serialize-only companion:",[337,744,746],{"className":405,"code":745,"language":407,"meta":342,"style":342},"@JsonProperty(\"eventTime\")           \u002F\u002F stays queryable as eventTime\nprivate Instant eventTime;\n\n\u002F\u002F emitted as @timestamp on write, ignored on read (eventTime is restored from its own key)\n@JsonProperty(value = \"@timestamp\", access = JsonProperty.Access.READ_ONLY)\npublic Instant esTimestamp() { return eventTime; }\n",[285,747,748,753,758,762,767,772],{"__ignoreMap":342},[346,749,750],{"class":348,"line":349},[346,751,752],{},"@JsonProperty(\"eventTime\")           \u002F\u002F stays queryable as eventTime\n",[346,754,755],{"class":348,"line":355},[346,756,757],{},"private Instant eventTime;\n",[346,759,760],{"class":348,"line":361},[346,761,445],{"emptyLinePlaceholder":444},[346,763,764],{"class":348,"line":367},[346,765,766],{},"\u002F\u002F emitted as @timestamp on write, ignored on read (eventTime is restored from its own key)\n",[346,768,769],{"class":348,"line":373},[346,770,771],{},"@JsonProperty(value = \"@timestamp\", access = JsonProperty.Access.READ_ONLY)\n",[346,773,774],{"class":348,"line":379},[346,775,776],{},"public Instant esTimestamp() { return eventTime; }\n",[265,778,720,779,782,783,395,786,788,789,794],{},[316,780,781],{},"duplicates"," the value (both ",[285,784,785],{},"eventTime",[285,787,631],{}," are stored). It is the same document shape that application entities get from the ",[269,790,791],{"href":98},[285,792,793],{},"@TimeReference"," decorator. When a migration creates the stream backing such an entity, declare the pairing so the stream documents which column is the natural time field:",[337,796,798],{"className":339,"code":797,"language":341,"meta":342,"style":342},"CREATE DATA STREAM sensor_readings (\n    id        KEYWORD,\n    eventTime DATE,\n    value     DOUBLE\n) WITH (DATA_RETENTION = '30d', TIME_REFERENCE = 'eventTime') ;\n",[285,799,800,805,810,815,820],{"__ignoreMap":342},[346,801,802],{"class":348,"line":349},[346,803,804],{},"CREATE DATA STREAM sensor_readings (\n",[346,806,807],{"class":348,"line":355},[346,808,809],{},"    id        KEYWORD,\n",[346,811,812],{"class":348,"line":361},[346,813,814],{},"    eventTime DATE,\n",[346,816,817],{"class":348,"line":367},[346,818,819],{},"    value     DOUBLE\n",[346,821,822],{"class":348,"line":373},[346,823,824],{},") WITH (DATA_RETENTION = '30d', TIME_REFERENCE = 'eventTime') ;\n",[265,826,827,830,831,834,835,837,838,840],{},[285,828,829],{},"TIME_REFERENCE"," must name a declared ",[285,832,833],{},"DATE"," column; the migration validates this and fails fast otherwise. It declares intent and pairs with the entity's ",[285,836,793],{}," — the write-time duplication into ",[285,839,631],{}," is performed by the persistence layer, not the migration.",[329,842,844],{"id":843},"writing-and-reading","Writing and reading",[265,846,847,848,851,852,855,856,859],{},"Data streams are ",[316,849,850],{},"append-only",": Elasticsearch accepts only the ",[285,853,854],{},"create"," op-type and rejects the ",[285,857,858],{},"index"," op and by-id updates\u002Fdeletes. Documents are retrieved by search\u002Fquery, not by id. System services that write to a stream must therefore use a create (append) write, and read through search rather than a find-by-id lookup.",[861,862,863],"style",{},"html .light .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-light);background: var(--shiki-light-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-light-font-style);font-weight: var(--shiki-light-font-weight);text-decoration: var(--shiki-light-text-decoration);}html.light .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-light);background: var(--shiki-light-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-light-font-style);font-weight: var(--shiki-light-font-weight);text-decoration: var(--shiki-light-text-decoration);}html .default .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-default);background: var(--shiki-default-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-default-font-style);font-weight: var(--shiki-default-font-weight);text-decoration: var(--shiki-default-text-decoration);}html .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-default);background: var(--shiki-default-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-default-font-style);font-weight: var(--shiki-default-font-weight);text-decoration: var(--shiki-default-text-decoration);}html .dark .shiki span {color: var(--shiki-dark);background: var(--shiki-dark-bg);font-style: var(--shiki-dark-font-style);font-weight: var(--shiki-dark-